1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:09,200
A thousand examples can suggest a rule. One counterexample can change
it. This is Counterseed: a graph laboratory for finding the beautiful
exception.

2
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:16,765
Instead of asking a chatbot whether a conjecture is true, we write a
precise question, search a complete finite universe,

3
00:00:16,765 --> 00:00:23,952
and keep evidence that anyone can replay. Everything runs in the
browser, without an account or an API key.

4
00:00:25,452 --> 00:00:34,769
Here is a tempting conjecture: if a graph has no triangles, then it must
be bipartite. Counterseed checks graphs in a declared order:

5
00:00:34,769 --> 00:00:41,655
first by vertices, then by edges. It finds the smallest exception after
five hundred and seven graphs:

6
00:00:41,655 --> 00:00:50,161
a five-cycle. There are no triangles, but an odd cycle cannot be colored
with just two colors. The premise is true.

7
00:00:50,161 --> 00:00:54,616
The conclusion is false. One tiny graph breaks the universal claim.

8
00:00:56,116 --> 00:01:05,427
The witness is not a picture pasted into a demo. Remove one edge and the
actual graph changes. So do its chromatic number,

9
00:01:05,427 --> 00:01:12,308
girth, degree sequence, and both sides of the implication. The cycle
becomes a path, which is bipartite.

10
00:01:12,308 --> 00:01:18,785
Restore the witness, drag the layout, or edit any vertex pair with the
accessible adjacency controls.

11
00:01:18,785 --> 00:01:23,237
Twenty-four exact properties let us inspect the structure behind the
answer.

12
00:01:24,737 --> 00:01:34,009
Now we can ask something different. Does a connected graph with at least
two neighbors at every vertex have no bridges? The answer

13
00:01:34,009 --> 00:01:42,072
is no. Counterseed finds two triangles joined by a single fragile
connection. Remove that bridge and the graph splits apart.

14
00:01:42,072 --> 00:01:48,522
This is a custom logical expression, not a hard-coded result. The safe
property language supports AND,

15
00:01:48,522 --> 00:01:53,762
OR, NOT, parentheses, and numerical comparisons, so students can build
their own experiments.

16
00:01:55,262 --> 00:02:04,695
Some conjectures survive. For connected graphs with even degrees, the
Eulerian test constructs a walk that uses every edge exactly once and
returns

17
00:02:04,695 --> 00:02:12,486
to its starting point. Here we check all thirty-three thousand, eight
hundred and sixty-seven labeled graphs through six vertices.

18
00:02:12,486 --> 00:02:21,919
No counterexample appears. The interface says bounded evidence, not a
theorem. Checking every small graph does not prove a statement about all
graphs.

19
00:02:23,419 --> 00:02:33,929
Every experiment leaves a paper trail. Field notes preserve the question
and the finite universe. The downloadable record includes the search
order,

20
00:02:33,929 --> 00:02:43,962
counts, smallest witness, exact properties, and a reproducible
fingerprint. A separate command-line verifier uses independently written
graph algorithms to replay it.

21
00:02:43,962 --> 00:02:53,040
Core properties were checked against independent oracles across the
entire six-vertex universe. Counterseed is an original Open Invention
project,

22
00:02:53,040 --> 00:02:58,295
built with disclosed AI assistance. Good questions grow from small
exceptions.
